; A `handle` NAMES an effect and DISCHARGES a different set, and the
; difference is the whole of this case.
;
; AX3011 asks whether the list names what the body performs, and it has
; to be exhaustive: `320-unhandled-effect.ax` is the bank of refusals
; for a list that omits something. That check makes the list a
; description of the region rather than a wish, and it means a
; built-in
; in the list is never an accident - the author had to write `(IO)` for
; the form to compile at all.
;
; Propagation asks a different question: does the effect still happen
; to the CALLER. For a declared effect the answer is no, because a
; handler is installed and really does intercept the operations. For a
; built-in there is no handler - `handleIsDynamic` installs evidence
; only for a declared effect, and the form otherwise lowers to its body
; alone - so the syscall runs and the answer is yes.
;
; Until 2026-08-23 both questions were answered by the same predicate,
; so naming a built-in erased it from everything above the handle:
;
;   `launders` claims `pure`, acknowledges IO, and reported `#pure`
;   while writing to stdout. The claim check saw nothing to contradict.
;
;   `caller` truthfully claims `effect(io)` - it calls a function that
;   performs I/O - and drew the ONLY diagnostic in the file, for
;   claiming an effect the erasure had removed. The honest one was
;   warned and the lying one passed.
;
; The diagnostic is on `launders` now and `caller` is silent.
;
; `discharges` is the CONTROL, and it earns its place twice. Its
; handler is real, so `Ask` genuinely does not reach its caller: a fix
; that simply stopped subtracting everything would report a missing
; `Ask` here and does not. And its claim is `effect(alloc)`, not
; `pure`, because installing a handler ALLOCATES - the evidence record
; is a real block - so the honest claim for a function that discharges
; a declared effect names Alloc. That `discharges` draws nothing while
; `launders` draws AX3010 is the whole distinction, stated as two
; lines of one file.
(effect Ask
  (ask :: (-> Int Int)))

(:: shout (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (shout n)
  {
    (__syscall3 1 n 0 0)
    n
  }
)

(:: asker (-> Int Int))

(fn (asker n) (ask n))

(:: launders (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:pure
(fn (launders n)
  (handle (shout n) (IO) 0)
)

(:: discharges (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(alloc)
(fn (discharges n)
  (handle (asker n) (Ask) (lambda (v) 0))
)

; Two shapes that are NOT about a false claim, and are the reason this
; is a defect rather than a loophole for liars.
;
; `guarded` is named that and not `region` because `region` is a
; REMOVED keyword the formatter still knows: `check` accepts it as an
; ordinary name and `fmt` refuses the whole file, which is how
; `check-fmt.sh` reported this fixture's first draft. The compiler and
; its formatter disagree about what is an identifier, and that is worth
; a note here rather than a silent rename.
;
; `guarded` is the `(Pure)` boundary - the one effect construct in the
; language that refuses to emit - defeated by a `handle` INSIDE it: the
; inner form subtracted the IO before the outer one measured, so the
; strongest primitive the language has could be switched off by the
; code it was guarding. It is AX3011 now, an error, and no binary is
; written.
;
; `run` is honest, and was corrupted anyway. `(Ask IO)` - a declared
; effect beside a built-in the handler cannot intercept - is the shape
; `docs/reference.md` documents, and it silently erased the IO.
; Nothing here claims anything, so no diagnostic was possible and none
; was missing: the machine-readable answer was simply wrong, which is
; worse for a reader that is a program than a warning would be.
;
; That half is not visible in THIS file's goldens, and the reason is
; worth stating: `guarded` above makes the file a refusal, and
; `symbols` answers nothing for a file that does not check. Measured
; on the same shape standing alone - a body doing `log` and `println`
; under `(Ask IO)` - AXSYM read `#effects=Alloc` before and
; `#effects=Alloc,IO` after. `run` stays here because it is the
; propagation this fix changes, and because a reader comparing the two
; halves of the file should see that the erasure never depended on
; anyone lying.
(:: guarded (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:pure
(fn (guarded n)
  (handle (handle (shout n) (IO) 0) (Pure) 0)
)

(:: logger (-> Int Int))

(fn (logger n)
  {
    (ask n)
    (shout n)
  }
)

(:: run (-> Int Int))

(fn (run n)
  (handle (logger n) (Ask IO) (lambda (v) 0))
)

(:: caller (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (caller n) (launders n))

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main) (+ (+ (caller 1) (discharges 2)) (+ (guarded 3) (run 4))))
